-- ===== Sync the KB edit onto rows already inserted by an earlier run of this migration =====
update questions set data = jsonb_set(data, '{explanation}', to_jsonb('<p style="margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)">Opioid-induced immunosuppression depends on structural features of the drug (hydroxyl groups at C3 and C6, and receptor affinity), is reversed by receptor antagonism, and is distinct from the immune suppression caused by uncontrolled pain itself.</p><p style="margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> &mdash; Morphine retains hydroxyl groups at both C3 and C6 and has high affinity for the opioid receptor, making it among the most immunosuppressive opioids. Methadone, fentanyl, remifentanil, and pethidine also cause immunosuppression, though to a lesser degree.</p><p style="margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> &mdash; Modification at the C6 position abolishes the immunosuppressive effect: opioids such as hydromorphone, oxycodone, and tramadol (which have C6 modification) show no significant immunosuppressive effect, whereas modification at C3 alone (e.g. codeine, dihydrocodeine) only reduces, rather than abolishes, the effect.</p><p style="margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> &mdash; Buprenorphine has lower affinity for the opioid receptor and is associated with less immunosuppressive effect compared with high-affinity opioids like morphine; it is not classed as markedly immunosuppressive.</p><p style="margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> &mdash; Naloxone and naltrexone (opioid receptor antagonists) enhance immune responses, consistent with the concept that opioid receptor activation itself contributes to immunosuppression, which is reversed/blocked by antagonism.</p><p style="margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> &mdash; It is uncontrolled pain &mdash; not reduced pain &mdash; that decreases NK cell activity, as does the surgical stress response via the sympathetic nervous system and HPA axis. Effective analgesia reducing pain would therefore be expected to help preserve, not further decrease, NK cell activity, though the opioids used to achieve that analgesia may independently suppress immune function via their own receptor-mediated mechanisms.</p><div style="margin-top:18px"><div style="border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)">
    <span style="font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px">Opioids and Immunosuppression</span>
    <div style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0">
      <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/></svg>
      <span style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px">Knowledge</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div style="background:var(--surface-warm)"><div style="padding:16px 18px 14px;"><p style="margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)">Opioids modulate the immune system both directly, by binding to &mu;-opioid receptors expressed on immune cells, and indirectly, via activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis and the sympathetic nervous system (SNS). This releases glucocorticoids and noradrenaline, both of which act on leucocytes to reduce immune responsiveness.</p></div><div style="padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);"><div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px">Evidence Base</div><table style="margin:0"><tr><th>Source</th><th>Finding</th></tr><tr><td>Animal</td><td>Accelerated onset of simian immunodeficiency virus (SIV) in opioid-treated animals</td></tr><tr><td>Human</td><td>Higher rates of malignancy and faster tumour spread reported in opioid users</td></tr></table></div><div style="padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);"><div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px">Structure&#8211;Activity Relationship</div><div style="text-align:center"><img src="images/opioid-c3-c6-hydroxyl.svg" alt="Morphinan skeleton showing the C3 and C6 hydroxyl positions that determine opioid immunosuppressive potential" style="max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:4px;border:1px solid #EDE3D7"></div></div><div style="padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);"><div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px">Opioid Antagonists and Peripheral Mechanisms</div><p style="margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)">Naloxone and naltrexone <strong>enhance</strong> immune responses, consistent with opioid receptor activation itself driving immunosuppression. Peripherally, endogenous opioids inhibit IL-2 production, while exogenous opioids inhibit IL-10 and IL-12 production by macrophages &mdash; increasing susceptibility to bacterial and viral infection &mdash; and morphine reduces bone-marrow macrophage production and T-lymphocyte proliferation.</p></div><div style="padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);"><div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px">TLR4, Microglia, and Central Effects</div><p style="margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)">Morphine (and, to varying degrees, other opioids) acts as an agonist at Toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4), an innate immune pattern-recognition receptor normally responding to bacterial lipopolysaccharide. TLR4 is expressed on spinal cord microglia and other glial cells, and opioid-induced activation triggers a pro-inflammatory glial response (TNF-&alpha;, IL-1&beta;, IL-6, reactive oxygen species) implicated in three clinically important phenomena:</p><table style="margin:12px 0 0"><tr><th>Phenomenon</th><th>Contribution of glial activation</th></tr><tr><td>Opioid tolerance</td><td>Counteracts and reduces analgesic effect over time, driving dose escalation</td></tr><tr><td>Opioid-induced hyperalgesia</td><td>Contributes to paradoxical pain sensitisation</td></tr><tr><td>Withdrawal severity</td><td>Implicated in withdrawal symptom generation</td></tr></table></div><div style="padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);"><div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px">Tumour Spread, NK Cells, and Clinical Relevance</div><p style="margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)">Natural killer (NK) cells provide immunosurveillance against malignant cells. <strong>Uncontrolled/severe pain</strong> reduces NK cell activity, as does the surgical stress response via the SNS and HPA axis &mdash; providing a theoretical rationale for effective analgesia and opioid-sparing/regional techniques in cancer surgery. This gives a theoretical basis for preferring lower-immunosuppression opioids (e.g. oxycodone, tramadol, buprenorphine) in patients with malignancy or immunocompromise, though robust outcome data remain limited; adequate analgesia itself is still important, since uncontrolled pain independently suppresses NK activity.</p></div></div>
</div></div>'::text))
  where id = 'q_mtf_opioids_immunosuppression_001';
update questions set data = jsonb_set(data, '{explanation}', to_jsonb('<p style="margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)">Opioid-induced immunosuppression is related to structural features of the opioid molecule &mdash; specifically the presence of hydroxyl groups at the C3 and C6 positions, alongside high mu-opioid receptor affinity. Modification at the C6 position abolishes this immunosuppressive effect, making C6-modified opioids such as oxycodone, hydromorphone, and tramadol theoretically preferable where minimising immunosuppression is a clinical priority.</p><p style="margin:12px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Morphine retains hydroxyl groups at both C3 and C6 and has high receptor affinity, making it among the most immunosuppressive opioids &mdash; the least suitable choice here.</p><p style="margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Methadone causes immunosuppression, though less marked than morphine, but is not the C6-modified, minimally immunosuppressive option represented by oxycodone.</p><p style="margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Fentanyl is also associated with an immunosuppressive effect, albeit less marked than morphine, and does not carry the specific C6 modification that abolishes this effect.</p><p style="margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Pethidine similarly causes a degree of immunosuppression, less marked than morphine but still present, and lacks the structural modification associated with abolition of the effect.</p><p style="margin:8px 0 0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)"><strong>E. Correct.</strong> Oxycodone has modification at the C6 position, which abolishes the immunosuppressive effect seen with morphine and other unmodified opioids, making it the most appropriate choice among those listed when minimising immunosuppressive potential is a specific clinical consideration.</p><div style="margin-top:18px"><div style="border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)">
    <span style="font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px">Opioids and Immunosuppression</span>
