      <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/></svg>
      <span style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px">Knowledge</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div style="background:var(--surface-warm)"><div style="padding:16px 18px 14px;"><p style="margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)">Opioids modulate the immune system both directly, by binding to &mu;-opioid receptors expressed on immune cells, and indirectly, via activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis and the sympathetic nervous system (SNS). This releases glucocorticoids and noradrenaline, both of which act on leucocytes to reduce immune responsiveness.</p></div><div style="padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);"><div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px">Evidence Base</div><table style="margin:0"><tr><th>Source</th><th>Finding</th></tr><tr><td>Animal</td><td>Accelerated onset of simian immunodeficiency virus (SIV) in opioid-treated animals</td></tr><tr><td>Human</td><td>Higher rates of malignancy and faster tumour spread reported in opioid users</td></tr></table></div><div style="padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);"><div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px">Structure&#8211;Activity Relationship</div><div style="text-align:center"><img src="images/opioid-c3-c6-hydroxyl.svg" alt="Morphinan skeleton showing the C3 and C6 hydroxyl positions that determine opioid immunosuppressive potential" style="max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:4px;border:1px solid #EDE3D7"></div></div><div style="padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);"><div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px">Opioid Antagonists and Peripheral Mechanisms</div><p style="margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)">Naloxone and naltrexone <strong>enhance</strong> immune responses, consistent with opioid receptor activation itself driving immunosuppression. Peripherally, endogenous opioids inhibit IL-2 production, while exogenous opioids inhibit IL-10 and IL-12 production by macrophages &mdash; increasing susceptibility to bacterial and viral infection &mdash; and morphine reduces bone-marrow macrophage production and T-lymphocyte proliferation.</p></div><div style="padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);"><div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px">TLR4, Microglia, and Central Effects</div><p style="margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)">Morphine (and, to varying degrees, other opioids) acts as an agonist at Toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4), an innate immune pattern-recognition receptor normally responding to bacterial lipopolysaccharide. TLR4 is expressed on spinal cord microglia and other glial cells, and opioid-induced activation triggers a pro-inflammatory glial response (TNF-&alpha;, IL-1&beta;, IL-6, reactive oxygen species) implicated in three clinically important phenomena:</p><table style="margin:12px 0 0"><tr><th>Phenomenon</th><th>Contribution of glial activation</th></tr><tr><td>Opioid tolerance</td><td>Counteracts and reduces analgesic effect over time, driving dose escalation</td></tr><tr><td>Opioid-induced hyperalgesia</td><td>Contributes to paradoxical pain sensitisation</td></tr><tr><td>Withdrawal severity</td><td>Implicated in withdrawal symptom generation</td></tr></table></div><div style="padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);"><div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px">Tumour Spread, NK Cells, and Clinical Relevance</div><p style="margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)">Natural killer (NK) cells provide immunosurveillance against malignant cells. <strong>Uncontrolled/severe pain</strong> reduces NK cell activity, as does the surgical stress response via the SNS and HPA axis &mdash; providing a theoretical rationale for effective analgesia and opioid-sparing/regional techniques in cancer surgery. This gives a theoretical basis for preferring lower-immunosuppression opioids (e.g. oxycodone, tramadol, buprenorphine) in patients with malignancy or immunocompromise, though robust outcome data remain limited; adequate analgesia itself is still important, since uncontrolled pain independently suppresses NK activity.</p></div></div>
</div></div>'::text))
  where id = 'q_sba_opioid_choice_immunosuppression_malignancy_001';
